    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Primary osteoarthritis of both knees (M17.0)</content>. Dr. Lindqvist-Bamba agrees that the slower gait and the near-fall come mainly from knee osteoarthritis with quadriceps weakness and reduced balance, with diphenhydramine at night adding to the risk; there is no orthostatic fall, neuropathy, thyroid or B12 cause, and vision is adequate. The ACR clinical criteria are met, so no X-ray is needed. He is pre-frail (Clinical Frailty Scale 3, FRAIL 1 of 5). [S]<list><item>Diclofenac 1% gel to each painful knee up to four times a day from today.</item><item>Acetaminophen as needed within 3,000 mg a day.</item><item>Physical therapy for twelve weeks: a progressive strength and balance program based on Otago, then a tai chi class.</item><item>Home safety check by the physical therapist; well-fitting shoes with a back in the house.</item><item>DXA of the spine and hips before the eight-week review.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Potentially inappropriate over-the-counter medicines (Z79.1)</content>. Both stopped for two weeks without harm; sleep is recovering. [S]<list><item>No sleep aid; keep the sleep routine.</item><item>Check any over-the-counter medicine with the clinic or pharmacist.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Knee pain (0 to 10)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>4 of 10 on stairs, from 5</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Knee function (KOOS JR)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Up to date</td><td>62 at intake; next at eight weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Knee examination (range of movement, effusion, alignment, crepitus)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Bony enlargement, crepitus, flexion 115 degrees, no effusion; quadriceps 4 of 5</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Knee X-ray</td><td>Only for diagnostic uncertainty or surgical planning</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Clinical criteria met; no X-ray</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Exercise and self-management (strength and balance program, tai chi)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Physical therapy program set today</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Gait speed, Timed Up and Go, 30-second chair stand and four-stage balance test</td><td>At intake and after a strength and balance program</td><td>Up to date</td><td>At intake: gait speed 0.78 m/s, Timed Up and Go 13.8 seconds, 7 chair stands, tandem stance 6 seconds; repeat after the program</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Orthostatic blood pressure</td><td>At assessment and with any dizziness</td><td>Completed today</td><td>136/74 seated, 134/72 standing</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Feet and footwear</td><td>At assessment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Loose backless slippers; shoes with a back advised</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Home safety check</td><td>Once, after a fall or near-fall</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Home visit by the physical therapist in the next three weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medication review (Beers 2023): no NSAID tablets, sedating antihistamines or other anticholinergic medicines; over-the-counter purchases</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Naproxen and diphenhydramine stopped; nothing new</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Topical diclofenac: skin and use</td><td>At the start and every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Started today</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Vitamin B12</td><td>Once, for a gait disorder</td><td>Completed today</td><td>418 pg/mL, normal</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>25-hydroxyvitamin D</td><td>At baseline, after repletion, then yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>24 ng/mL at intake; vitamin D3 started; recheck after about three months</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>DXA and FRAX</td><td>At assessment, then every two years if untreated</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Booked before the eight-week review</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Frailty (Clinical Frailty Scale, FRAIL scale)</td><td>At assessment and yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Clinical Frailty Scale 3; FRAIL 1 of 5 (stairs): pre-frail</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>What matters most (4Ms)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>To walk into town to teach and to manage his stairs at home</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mentation: mood, memory and sleep (4Ms)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>MoCA 26 and GDS-15 2 at intake; sleeping six hours</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Geriatric medicine consultation</td><td>At assessment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Lindqvist-Bamba today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Find out why he walks more slowly and nearly fell. Outcome by 2026-03-16: Knee osteoarthritis with reduced quadriceps strength and balance, and diphenhydramine at night; no neurologic, thyroid, B12 or blood pressure cause [S]</item><item>Walk into town and back for his teaching without stopping to rest, and climb his stairs safely. In progress; target 2026-09-28 [S]</item><item>No medicine he does not need, and none on the Beers list. In progress; target 2026-04-27 [S]</item><item>Knee pain 3 of 10 or less on stairs. In progress; target 2026-06-29 [S]</item><item>Bones and falls: vitamin D above 30 ng/mL and no falls. In progress; target 2027-03-02 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Age-Friendly Health Systems: the 4Ms (What Matters, Medication, Mentation, Mobility) (2026); American Geriatrics Society 2023 updated AGS Beers Criteria for potentially inappropriate medication use in older adults (2023); Updating STEADI for Primary Care: Recommendations From the American Geriatrics Society Workgroup (2025); World guidelines for falls prevention and management for older adults: a global initiative (2022); Physical Frailty: ICFSR International Clinical Practice Guidelines for Identification and Management (2019); 2019 American College of Rheumatology/Arthritis Foundation Guideline for the Management of Osteoarthritis of the Hand, Hip, and Knee (2019); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis, and osteoporosis monographs (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Eight-week review after the DXA with a pain score and KOOS JR. Physical therapy weekly. The clinic for any fall. Next scheduled visit 2026-04-27: eight-week review: knee pain and function, the strength and balance program, sleep without diphenhydramine, the DXA result and pneumococcal vaccine. [S]</paragraph></text>

